There is no need for a URSF to prevent BIP110, and it's wild Chris believes you do. 

The mandatory lock-in ensures that standard nodes/miners will accept a BIP110 block and build ontop, but BIP110 nodes will reject a standard block.

The URSF is 'doing nothing', and BIP110 nodes will fork off as a result.

There is no game theory which supports miners switching to 110 at the last moment. These folks have convinced themselves that their crusade will go down exactly how SegWit did...It will not.

'“It ain’t what you don’t know that gets you into trouble.  It’s what you know for sure that just ain’t so.”

@OrangeSurfBTC has a tremendouly good tool to learn about how this all goes down: https://t.co/Q0g60CjkyD

P.S. If you are a BIP110er, please be careful with your funds around fork date. Your nodes will fall out of consensus. Whilst I oppose the BIP, I still don't want folks to lose your BTC because they didn't understand the technical details of a chainsplit.

For folks who genuinely believe you need a URSF to reject every soft fork proposal, just think about how fragile that is.

Some dude on the internet wants a new OP code --> we have to notice and code a URSF.

A government wants to restrict all transactions to halt the network --> we'd need to code up a URSF.

The whole crux of Bitcoin being resistant to change, is that the default answer to all fork proposals is NO, and the default behaviour of doing nothing literally resists all proposed changes.

